  "question": {
    "text": "A liquid of density <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>85</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi>cm</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></math> flows through a calorimeter at the rate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi>cm</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>. Heat is added by means of a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>250</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W</mi></math> electric heating coil and a temperature difference of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>15</mn><mo>°</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> is established in steady-state conditions between the inflow and the outflow points of the liquid. The specific heat for the liquid will be (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kcal</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>4186</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi></math>&nbsp;)",
    "image": null
